## 6️⃣ Test Way 6: NoSQL Injection (Registration Parameter Manipulation)
If the backend application relies on a NoSQL database structure (like MongoDB) to index and verify unique users, this replaces flat string fields with structured logical condition arrays to disrupt validation logic.

* 🎯 Target Parameters: email, username.
* 📋 Step-by-Step Execution:
1. Intercept a standard JSON registration payload in Burp Suite Proxy.
   2. Modify the JSON schema structure by introducing NoSQL conditional modifiers into the fields.
* 💉 Payloads:

{
  "username": "new_user_88",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "email": {"$ne": "existing_user@1win.com"}
}

* 🔍 Response Verification Criteria: Hit Send. If the server evaluates the conditional logic array instead of processing the entry as a strict string literal value, it can result in a database bypass or allow the account to overwrite or attach itself to an existing user's indexing scope. [20] 
* 💰 Policy Match / Reward: Injections (SQLi or equivalent) — $1,500 USD (Critical Severity) [21] 

